# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for leanprof's CI (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...).
# Lean's runtime is precompiled and not instrumented, but its calls to libc's
# memcpy, memmove and memset are intercepted. It hands Lean objects between
# threads, and mimalloc reuses their memory across threads, through atomics
# TSan cannot see, so its copies (lean_copy_expand_array into an array another
# worker freed) look like races. leanprof's runtime shares no memory through
# these functions: events and counters other threads read are written with
# atomics, and grown stacks are copied before they are published.
race_top:memcpy
race_top:memmove
race_top:memset
# the runtime's copies of arrays, scalar arrays and strings, by name (the
# interceptor's own name may not be what race_top sees)
race:lean_copy_
# Lean's C++ runtime (namespace lean::, which leanprof's C code is not in) and
# its IO primitives: a FILE buffer allocated by one task's read and freed by
# the object's finalizer on another thread, handed over through Lean objects
race:lean::
race:lean_io_prim_
# mimalloc, inside Lean's runtime (its per-thread data torn down at thread exit)
race:mi_
# a new object's header written by lean_alloc_ctor (inline, from lean.h) into
# memory another thread's object had before it was freed: mimalloc hands the
# block over through atomics TSan cannot see (taskchains: a task's context
# read by its worker, freed, and its block reused by the spawning thread)
race_top:lean_alloc_ctor_memory
# The same allocation/reuse report can be attributed to the inlined header
# initializer instead (CI run 40045798658, taskchains). The write is inside
# lean_alloc_ctor before the new context is published; the previous reader is
# lpf_task_run on the old context. Keep this limited to header initialization,
# rather than suppressing lpf_task_wrap/lpf_task_run or all leanprof frames.
race_top:lean_set_st_header
# And to the inlined field stores that fill the new context (CI run on
# review-fix-mcp 7533841, taskchains: lean_ctor_set in lpf_task_wrap against
# lean_ctor_get in lpf_task_run on the previous context in the same block).
# Suppressing frame by frame keeps missing the next inlining, so cover the
# function itself: lpf_task_wrap only fills the context it has just
# allocated, before publishing it through the task, so a real race on its
# stores would need a second writer it does not have.
race:lpf_task_wrap
